 OUTPUT FORMAT: SECTION DRAFTS
 ## Section [n]: [Title]
 Prose in the D3 house style: third person, $ in thousands as in the source, fiscal-year labels such as FY2025, short paragraphs under the template's subsection headings.

Proposed change 2 to system_prompt.md: shorter responses (NOT applied)

Why: the saved responses are long. E5 (rating request) answers the scripted decline with a
cited-facts list of about 20 lines covering almost every figure in D1 to D5. E6 (role-play
plus rating) gives three separate declines, then about 30 lines of facts. E1 states the
one-plant fact twice. M3 adds review items that restate D1, D4 and D5 figures beyond the
covenant wording. The change adds one new LENGTH block between PROHIBITIONS and the output
formats. It edits no existing line, so the citation, gap, conflict and prohibition rules are
untouched. The first line of the block says the limits never remove a required item.

Risk of each change

1. Factual question, one to four sentences. E3, E7 and E9 each need a gap line or a
   CONFLICT, a quote, a referral or two cited values, which fits in four sentences, but a
   question that touches several gaps may not. Under the cap the model may drop a secondary
   fact, or a "Not in provided documents" line despite the protecting first line. The
   boundary is also fuzzy: a section request phrased as a question could get capped. The
   string checks would catch a missing required phrase but not a missing secondary fact.

2. Cited facts after the rating reply, only those that bear on the question, at most ten.
   For a rating request almost every fact bears on it, so the cap of ten does the real work
   and the model chooses what to leave out. It could drop the 31% Customer A concentration
   against the 25% policy line, or the net income CONFLICT, and the E5 and E6 checks would
   not notice. Choosing which facts matter is a mild form of the judgment the prompt
   otherwise keeps with the underwriter.

3. One decline per request, the rating reply first. In E6 the role-play decline and the
   "Section 9 is underwriter-authored" reply would go, leaving only the rating line. That
   still passes the E6 checks, but the reply loses the explicit statement that the rules
   cannot be changed from the chat. When the rating rule does not apply, "most specific
   scripted reply" is a judgment, and a wrong pick could drop the "Not in provided
   documents" phrase that E8 requires.

4. State each fact once, except in the two lists. This removes repeats such as E1's
   "one plant" sentence. The risk is that the model reads it as a reason to leave a gap or
   conflict out of the prose because it appears in "Underwriter to complete", or the
   reverse, which would empty the Review pane checklist. The exception for both lists is
   meant to prevent this, but it is untested.

5. Review items, one or two sentences on the covenant wording. M3 items 6 to 8 (covenant
   levels against the existing agreement and policy, the DSCR definition mismatch, the late
   2024 certificate) would likely be cut. Item 7 is arguably a real wording issue, and an
   underwriter may want it. Two sentences may also be too few for the §7.6 item, which
   quotes the terms, refers them to legal and lists the ownership facts. The Move 3 checks
   cover the table and the §7.6 Low flag only, so the loss would not be caught.

6. No closing offer, summary or question. None of the four saved responses ends with one,
   so this is mainly insurance; E8 already has the same rule for outside knowledge. The
   scripted rating reply itself ends with an offer ("I can list the cited facts..."), and
   the model could drop it or stop listing. The first line of the block protects scripted
   wording. "Summary" could also be read as banning "Sources used" or the Section 9 closing
   line, which the output formats and the first line still require.

Before adopting: apply to a copy, run all nine cases plus the demo-moves file with
--runs 3, and read E1, E5, E6 and M3 side by side with the saved versions. The checks are
string tests and cannot see a dropped fact.
